/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An off-line UPS, also called standby, leaves the load on the mains in normal operation.
   - A line-interactive UPS adds a tap-changing transformer or buck-boost converter that corrects mains voltage while the load stays on the mains.
   - A static bypass is a fast switch, usually thyristors, from mains to load for inverter overload or fault.
   - UPS rating is in VA because inverter and transformer heating depends on current, and load current depends on apparent power S.
   - S = P / cos φ; Pdc = P / η; E = Vbat × Ah × DoD; t = E / Pdc
   - S is apparent power in VA; P is real load power in W; cos φ is load power factor; Pdc is battery-side DC power in W; η is inverter efficiency; Vbat is nominal battery voltage in V; Ah is battery capacity in ampere-hours; DoD is depth of dis
   - Battery sizing uses usable energy, not the printed Ah alone.
   - A linear supply steps down and rectifies the mains, then drops the excess voltage across a series transistor; it is clean but bulky and low efficiency.
*/
